  "query_preview": "..\nToday through Wednesday: A shallow ridge will briefly be over \nthe region today. Starting on Monday, A trough will be digging a\nlow along to coast into California. The Low becomes cutoff and \na split flow pattern develops over the Northwest. It will bring \ndry, warm pattern over the Inland Northwest. Precip chances \nacross the region remain near 0 percent through Wednesday. The \nregion can expect warm daytime and cold overnight conditions. \nWinds will be generally calm except a brief period of breezy \nwinds down the Okanogan Valley on Tuesday with gusts into the \nupper 20 mph range. Sunday highs will be 50s and low 60s and \nslightly increase through midweek into the 60s. Overnight lows \nwill be in the upper 20s and 30s. Overnight patchy frost could \nimpact any remaining gardens and sensitive seasonal plants. \n\nThursday through Saturday: The Low finally ejects northeast as \na trough moves into the Pacific Northwest. A southwest flow \npattern will push moisture into the region and bring increasing \nprecip chances late Wednesday through the Saturday. Ensembles \nhave increased precip chances across the region with a 10 \npercent probability of at least a hundredth by Friday. For a \ntenth of an inch, the Cascade Crest, extreme eastern Washington,\nthe Idaho Panhandle with at least a 20 percent probability. \nSnow levels dropping down to around 5,000 feet Thursday night. \nThe region will be under a moist trough pattern through the rest\nof the weekend. Shower chances are expected to continue into \nthe start of the work week. Highs will be in the 50s to low 60s.\nOvernight lows will be in the upper 20s and 30s. /JDC",
